Here's how to implement this in .NET MAUI Shell:

## 1. Override `OnBackButtonPressed` on your page

```csharp
public partial class EditProductPage : ContentPage
{
    private bool _hasUnsavedChanges;

    protected override bool OnBackButtonPressed()
    {
        if (_hasUnsavedChanges)
        {
            // Fire-and-forget the async dialog
            MainThread.BeginInvokeOnMainThread(async () =>
            {
                bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
                    "Unsaved Changes",
                    "You have unsaved changes. Discard and go back?",
                    "Discard", "Stay");

                if (leave)
                    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
            });

            return true; // Cancel the default back navigation
        }

        return false; // Allow normal navigation
    }
}
```

> `OnBackButtonPressed` handles the **hardware/system back button** (Android back gesture, etc.). Return `true` to cancel navigation.

## 2. Handle the Shell navigation bar back button

Shell's toolbar back arrow doesn't trigger `OnBackButtonPressed`. Hide it and add your own:

```csharp
public partial class EditProductPage : ContentPage
{
    public EditProductPage()
    {
        InitializeComponent();

        // Hide the default back button
        Shell.SetBackButtonBehavior(this, new BackButtonBehavior
        {
            IsVisible = false
        });
    }
}
```

## 3. Add a custom "Cancel" toolbar button

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="MyApp.EditProductPage">

    <Shell.TitleView>
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">
            <Button Text="Cancel"
                    Clicked="OnCancelClicked"
                    BackgroundColor="Transparent"
                    TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light=Black, Dark=White}" />
            <Label Grid.Column="1" Text="Edit Product"
                   VerticalOptions="Center" FontAttributes="Bold" />
        </Grid>
    </Shell.TitleView>

    <!-- page content -->
</ContentPage>
```

```csharp
private async void OnCancelClicked(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    if (_hasUnsavedChanges)
    {
        bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes",
            "Discard changes?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (!leave) return;
    }

    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
}
```

## Alternative: Use `BackButtonBehavior.Command` directly

Instead of hiding + custom TitleView, you can replace the back button behavior in one shot:

```csharp
Shell.SetBackButtonBehavior(this, new BackButtonBehavior
{
    TextOverride = "Cancel",
    Command = new Command(async () =>
    {
        if (!_hasUnsavedChanges)
        {
            await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
            return;
        }

        bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes", "Discard changes?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (leave)
            await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
    })
});
```

This replaces the arrow with a "Cancel" label and intercepts the tap — no need for a custom `TitleView`.

---

**Key points:**
- `OnBackButtonPressed` → system/hardware back (Android)
- `BackButtonBehavior` → Shell toolbar back button
- You need both to fully guard navigation
